issue_comments.body:
@simondanielsson thanks for the review! All inline comments are addressed in 4b7255a; replies are in the threads.

**Tests:** trimmed to five unit tests and two multi-GPU cases:
- score kernel vs a reference;
- grid-size invariance under CUDA graphs;
- profiling-run safety with an empty block table;
- per-head merge with 4 heads;
- fp8 rejection;
- CP vs non-CP top-k per head at TP=4 (spec-decode rows over RCCL, single-token decode over AITER).

The default-off and other config tests are gone.

**ISL sweep:** queued on our MI350X node as a `vllm bench serve` random-dataset sweep: ISL 4k–128k × concurrency 8 and 32, baseline vs CP in ABBA order. I'll post the table here when it's done. For reference, on the AgentX traces (average ISL ~74k) CP was ahead at every concurrency we measured (15 to 32: +2.7% to +15.2%), but that doesn't yet answer the short-input, low-concurrency case you're asking about.

@ChuanLi1101 Simon suggests auto-enabling CP whenever it's eligible (bf16, TP ≥ 2) if the sweep shows it's always faster. You'd asked earlier to keep it default-off. Since bf16 is M3's default index dtype, auto-enabling would turn it on for anyone not using fp8. Unless you'd prefer otherwise, I'll keep it off by default and add a recipe note for bf16 users above the ISL where the sweep shows a gain. Does that work for you?
